=== Step 2: your choices ===
  [ok] model: Qwen3.8-Flash-Next

  1) Q2_0     2-bit, the fastest; download 66 GB, uses ~34 GB of RAM   <- needs 48 GB RAM, you have 47
  2) IQ2_XS   2-bit i-quant, a little better quality, close in speed; download 68 GB, uses ~36 GB of RAM   <- needs 48 GB RAM, you have 47
  3) IQ3_XXS  3-bit i-quant, better quality, slower (more CPU work per token); download 76 GB, uses ~43 GB of RAM   <- fits in the low-RAM mode (the GPU holds ~44%, the rest in RAM)
  4) IQ3_S    3.5-bit i-quant, the best quality (matches the full model), the slowest; needs a 64 GB PC with little else running; download 84 GB, uses ~50 GB of RAM   <- fits in the low-RAM mode (the GPU holds ~38%, the rest in RAM)
  [!]  the low-RAM mode on GPU 0 (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090, 24 GB) + GPU 1 (NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3090, 24 GB), as you chose (--gpus): the experts the GPUs do not hold are read through the OS file cache (the resident variant has no layer split yet), and RAM can fill up to 0 free during long prompts
       One GPU keeps them in RAM (steady RAM use, recommended): --gpu 0
  [ok] size: IQ3_XXS
  [ok] context: 131072 tokens
  [ok] KV cache: 8-bit
  [ok] images: off
  [ok] low-RAM mode on 2 GPUs: IQ3_XXS's experts (43 GB) are read from the model folder through the OS file cache instead of a copy in RAM (47 GB); the GPUs hold ~82% of them

  EXPERIMENTAL - speed projection: a small control vector applied while the model runs (layers 4-44).
  It changes how the model answers: its package describes it as a refusal-direction projection (the
  model declines far fewer requests). Off unless you choose it; when on, the web app can switch it off
  per chat. Details: data/experimental-speed-projection/README.md
  [ok] experimental speed projection: off

